# Template for `secrets.env` (gitignored). `post-start` writes the real file
# by exporting from Infisical (.devcontainer/script/get-secrets-infisical.sh);
# this template documents its shape for anyone bootstrapping without
# Infisical, and is the single source `hsm-app-api`/`hsm-app-web`/
# `hsm-app-worker` read via `env_file: ../secrets.env` in
# docker/docker-compose.yaml (rewrite plan U17, Task 22).
#
# Two key families below:
#   - The legacy frozen-system keys (DB_*, JWT_*, SMTP_*, STRG_*, ...) —
#     carried over from the TypeScript system's Infisical secret set.
#   - The ASP.NET Core configuration keys the .NET hosts actually bind
#     (double-underscore = colon-nested config, e.g. `ConnectionStrings__HsmDb`
#     binds `ConnectionStrings:HsmDb`). These are what the compose app
#     services need; values below point at the compose network's own service
#     names (postgres, redis, rustfs, meilisearch, otel-collector) — NEVER
#     the dev-container-published localhost ports, which are not reachable
#     from inside another compose service's network namespace.
#
# hsm-app-api and hsm-app-web MUST receive identical Auth__CookieSecure /
# Auth__CookieDomain values (rewrite plan follow-up, CF20): both write
# Hsm.Contracts.Auth.AuthCookiePolicy's cookies, so a divergent posture
# between them breaks cross-host cookie validation. Sharing one secrets.env
# via env_file is what guarantees that by construction — do not override
# either key per-service.

# --- Legacy frozen-system keys (Infisical-managed; not all are read by the
#     .NET hosts yet) -------------------------------------------------------
# Placeholders below except where the value is already public in the tracked
# docker-compose.yaml (the postgres/redis/rustfs/meilisearch dev credentials
# it sets directly) — duplicating those here is not a new exposure.
